    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the \"small steps\" thing feels so hollow. like, what's the point of changing a routine when the person i did it all for is gone? every single thing i do or used to do was for them. without that, i feel like a shell. it's not even sadness anymore, it just feels like there's literally no reason to wake up and do it all again. it's just a void.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That feeling of inertia and purposelessness when your entire identity was tied to caregiving is incredibly painful. It makes perfect sense that routine feels hollow when the focus of your life has been removed. Instead of forcing a \"routine,\" perhaps try focusing only on the absolute bare necessities for yourself today, like a single walk or one cup of tea. Please remember that allowing yourself to feel the depth of this void is part of the grieving process, and you do not have to feel \"productive\" right now.",
      "source": "target",
